<section #sec:emphasis | Emphasis & Weight>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Enscribe keeps meaning and appearance apart. Use `<em>` and `<strong>` when you mean emphasis or importance; use italic or bold when you only want the slant or weight.

Emphasis (semantic): `<em | text>`
Strong (semantic): `<strong | text>`
Italic (visual): `<i | text>` or `*text*`
Bold (visual): `<b | text>` or `**text**`

<code #code:emphasis-basic>
This <em | really> matters, and this is <strong | critical>.
For plain styling, *this is italic* and **this is bold**.
</code>

<minipage #mp:emphasis-basic>
This <em | really> matters, and this is <strong | critical>.
For plain styling, *this is italic* and **this is bold**.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

`<i>`, `<b>`, and `<u>` take an optional `type` that records why the text is set apart:

<table csv -numbered>
i — type, foreign | taxonomic | technical | thought | ship-name | other
b — type, keyword | product-name | lead | offset | other
u — type, misspelling | proper-name | editorial-correction | other
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

The markdown `*`/`**` render as italic and bold (`<i>`/`<b>`), not as `<em>`/`<strong>`. When you mean emphasis, reach for the named tags — they carry the meaning into the XML and JATS, where bare italic and bold do not.
</section>

<section #sec:super-subscript | Superscript & Subscript>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Superscript: `<sup | text>` or `^{text}`
Subscript: `<sub | text>` or `_{text}`

<code #code:supsub-basic>
Water is H_{2}O and area grows as r^{2}.
The same in named form: H<sub | 2>O and r<sup | 2>.
</code>

<minipage #mp:supsub-basic>
Water is H_{2}O and area grows as r^{2}.
The same in named form: H<sub | 2>O and r<sup | 2>.
</minipage>
</section>

<section #sec:inline-marks | Other Inline Marks>

<sub-section | General Usage>

Quotation: `<q | text>`
Strikethrough: `<s | text>` or `~~text~~`
Underline: `<u | text>`
Abbreviation: `<abbr title="…" | text>`
Keyboard: `<kbd | text>`

<code #code:marks-basic>
She replied <q | of course> and crossed out ~~the old price~~.
Press <kbd | Ctrl+S> to save. The <abbr title="Document Object Model" | DOM>
is the browser's API for HTML.
</code>

<minipage #mp:marks-basic>
She replied <q | of course> and crossed out ~~the old price~~.
Press <kbd | Ctrl+S> to save. The <abbr title="Document Object Model" | DOM>
is the browser's API for HTML.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
abbr — title (the expansion), title="Document Object Model"
u — type, misspelling | proper-name | editorial-correction | other
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

Keep `~~` clear of `$` — a dollar sign opens inline math, which will swallow the run.
</section>

<section #sec:links | Links>

<sub-section | General Usage>

A link is `<a destination | link text>`. The destination is a full URL for an external link, or a page slug for an internal one — the build resolves slugs to their final paths.

<code #code:links-basic>
Read <a https://enscribe.org | the Enscribe site> for more,
or jump to <a vocabulary | the vocabulary page> in this document.
</code>

<minipage #mp:links-basic>
Read <a https://enscribe.org | the Enscribe site> for more,
or jump to <a vocabulary | the vocabulary page> in this document.
</minipage>

<sub-section | Arguments>

<table csv -numbered>
destination (URL or page slug), https://example.com  ·  vocabulary
Open in a new tab, target=_blank
rel (link relationship), nofollow | noopener | noreferrer | external
title (tooltip text), title="…"
Id, #a:docs
Class, .external
</table>

<sub-section | Notes>

Markdown `[text](url)` is not a register in Enscribe — write the link with `<a … | …>`.
</section>
